{"kind":"artifact","text":"Dual-era MCP service at c8d0afaa, unmerged on branch task/mcp-dual-era. Closes the finding in 21cc0b2d: a second initialize after a valid one was accepted and renegotiated the version, so a client told it was speaking 2025-06-18 could send another handshake and be answered 2024-11-05, silently changing what the connection is. My rule was too narrow — it refused initialize only after modern traffic, because that was the case the previous review raised, leaving a legacy connection able to reopen itself indefinitely. Initialization opens a connection: it comes first, and once. The refusal now keys on the era being settled at all, which covers both the crossed-era case and the repeated one, and neither refusal disturbs what was already negotiated. This is the third finding in a row where my validation was right for the case named and wrong for the case adjacent to it, so the test is written against the property rather than the instance: the negotiated version must survive a second handshake. Confirmed to fail against 7fa16cc3, where the second initialize returned 2024-11-05, and to pass here. Verified live over stdio as well as in the suite — first handshake negotiates, second is refused with -32600, and the session keeps serving tools afterwards. make vet, make test, and go test -race ./... pass. Not reviewed at this head and not merged.","body":{"commit":"c8d0afaa89f2a1e48554a32491f6efeadf348623","path":"spike/cmd/gitseq-mcp"}}